insert into public.reading_passages (id, language, level, title, body, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-reading-en-b2-464bdbb9b2d4', 'en', 'B2', 'The Day the Test Was Delayed: A Story of Waiting and Worry', 'It was a tense Tuesday morning in May 2006. Thousands of high school seniors across California had just taken the California High School Exit Examination, a test they needed to pass to graduate. For many, this was their last chance. But on Monday, a judge had hinted that he might stop the test, and now everyone was waiting for his final decision.

The lawsuit had been filed by a law firm on behalf of a student named Liliana Valenzuela. She and others argued that the test unfairly affected poor students and English learners who didn''t have the same educational opportunities. The judge, Robert B. Freedman, had listened carefully, but he hadn''t made up his mind yet. He said he would announce his decision on Friday.

By Wednesday, the tension was palpable. At James Logan High School, thirty-two students were scheduled to take the second part of the test, along with thousands of others statewide. Many of these students had studied for weeks, but they still felt unprepared. They worried that if the test stood, they would leave school without a diploma, even after four years of hard work. The pressure was intensified by the fact that many of them had already failed the test once and were not confident they would do better this time. Their anxiety was evident in the hallways as they huddled in groups, whispering about the judge''s upcoming ruling and what might happen if they failed. Some of them had stayed up all night cramming, but they knew that no amount of last-minute studying could make up for years of inadequate schooling. Others had given up hope and were already looking into options like the California High School Proficiency Exam, which offered an alternative path to a diploma. The uncertainty of the situation weighed heavily on their minds, and they felt that their futures were being decided by forces beyond their control.

Meanwhile, state officials defended the test. They argued that it ensured students had basic skills in math and English. The superintendent who had written the law said that nearly 90% of seniors had already passed. But for the 47,000 who hadn''t, the stakes were incredibly high. He emphasized that the test was not meant to be a barrier, but a tool to ensure that students were ready for life after high school. He also pointed out that the test had been in place for several years and had already helped to raise graduation rates. However, critics of the test countered that the statistics were misleading because they did not account for the disproportionate impact on minority and low-income students. They argued that the test was a ''one-size-fits-all'' approach that ignored the complex realities of students'' lives. The debate raged on in the media, with parents, teachers, and politicians weighing in on both sides. In addition, some educators noted that the test measured only a narrow set of skills, and that many students who failed were actually capable of college-level work. They called for a more holistic assessment that considered factors such as attendance, grades, and teacher recommendations. This added another layer of complexity to an already heated controversy, as both sides sought to influence public opinion before the judge''s final ruling.

As the week dragged on, the students lived in a kind of limbo. They didn''t know whether to celebrate their test results or start planning for summer school. Some parents took time off work to wait with their kids. Teachers tried to keep the mood light, but even they felt the weight of the decision. The atmosphere in the classroom was subdued, and lessons were often interrupted by whispered conversations about the latest news. Some teachers tried to incorporate the situation into their lessons, using it as a real-world example of how the legal system works. Others avoided the topic altogether, fearing it would only increase the students'' stress. The students'' anxiety was compounded by the fact that they had no control over the outcome. They could only wait and hope that the judge would rule in their favor. At lunch, groups of students gathered around radios and smartphones, checking for any updates. Some of them called their parents to share rumors, while others tried to distract themselves with games or music. The uncertainty was exhausting, and many students reported difficulty sleeping at night, tossing and turning as they replayed the worst-case scenarios in their minds.

Finally, Friday arrived. The courtroom was packed, and the atmosphere was electric. The judge entered, and everyone held their breath. He cleared his throat and read his ruling. The test was allowed to stand for now, but the lawsuit would continue. For the students, it was a bittersweet outcome. They still had to pass the test, but they knew their fight wasn''t over. The ruling meant that they had to take the test as scheduled, but they could take comfort in the fact that the legal challenge was still alive. Some students were relieved that the test was not canceled outright, as they had studied for it and wanted the opportunity to pass. Others were disappointed that the judge had not stopped the test, as they felt that they were not ready. But regardless of their individual feelings, they all knew that the outcome was not final and that there was still hope for change in the future. As they left the courthouse, they were met by reporters asking for their reactions. A senior named Maria, who had been waiting anxiously with her mother, said, "We will keep fighting. This is not the end." Her words captured the mood of many who felt that the battle was far from over. The story would continue to unfold in the coming months, as the legal proceedings moved forward and the students faced their exams with a mix of determination and dread.', '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O processo alegava que o teste violava o conceito de ''proteção igual'' porque estudantes pobres e aprendizes de inglês não tinham acesso à mesma educação, tornando-o injusto para esses grupos.","options":["The test was too difficult for most students.","The test was unfair to certain groups of students.","The test was not required in other states.","The test was too expensive to administer."],"prompt":"What was the main issue in the lawsuit mentioned in the passage?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto menciona que cerca de 47.000 alunos do último ano do ensino médio na Califórnia ainda não tinham passado no teste e seriam afetados pela decisão.","options":["About 32 students","Nearly 90,000 students","47,000 students","Thousands of students"],"prompt":"How many students were affected by the possible decision?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Na segunda-feira, o juiz disse que emitiria uma liminar contra o teste, a menos que os advogados do estado apresentassem um argumento convincente até as 14h. Ele não emitiu a liminar imediatamente.","options":["He issued an injunction immediately.","He postponed the test until Friday.","He said he might stop the test unless the state argued convincingly.","He ruled against the students."],"prompt":"What was the judge''s initial action on Monday?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto menciona que dezenas de alunos da James Logan High School estavam entre os que ainda não haviam passado no exame e seriam barrados da formatura se não passassem.","options":["They were the ones who filed the lawsuit.","They were the first to take the test.","They were among those who needed to pass the test.","They had already passed the test."],"prompt":"Why were the students at James Logan High School mentioned?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O superintendente defendia o teste argumentando que ele garantia que os alunos tivessem habilidades básicas em matemática e inglês.","options":["The test was required by federal law.","The test helped ensure students had basic skills.","The test was easy for most students.","The test was popular among parents."],"prompt":"What was the superintendent''s argument in favor of the test?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Na sexta-feira, o juiz permitiu que o teste continuasse por enquanto, mas o processo continuaria. A decisão não foi definitiva.","options":["The test was canceled permanently.","The test was allowed for now, but the lawsuit continues.","The test was postponed for a year.","The lawsuit was dismissed."],"prompt":"What was the final decision announced on Friday?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto descreve os alunos vivendo em um ''limbo'', sem saber se deveriam comemorar os resultados ou planejar a recuperação, indicando ansiedade e incerteza.","options":["They were confident they would pass.","They were relaxed and carefree.","They were anxious and uncertain.","They were angry and protested."],"prompt":"How did the students feel during the week before the decision?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Alguns educadores notaram que o teste media apenas um conjunto restrito de habilidades e pediram uma avaliação mais holística que considerasse fatores como frequência, notas e recomendações de professores.","options":["They suggested eliminating all testing.","They suggested a more holistic assessment.","They suggested making the test easier.","They suggested postponing the test indefinitely."],"prompt":"What did some educators suggest as an alternative to the test?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
