insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b2-e029706295bb', 'en', 'B2', 'Past Perfect in Narratives: Sequencing Events Like a Pro', 'Have you ever told a story and realized that your listener got confused about the order of events? For example, you say, "I arrived at the party, and she left." Did she leave before or after you arrived? In English, the past perfect tense helps clear up such confusion. It allows you to show which action happened first when you''re talking about two or more past events. At B2 level, mastering this tense can significantly improve your storytelling and make your English sound more natural and precise.

Consider this scenario: you come home and find a note on the table. You might say, "When I arrived, she had left." Here, ''had left'' indicates that she left before I arrived. Without the past perfect, the relationship is ambiguous. The past perfect is formed with ''had'' plus the past participle of the verb (e.g., ''had eaten'', ''had seen''). It''s often used with time expressions like ''before'', ''after'', ''by the time'', and ''already'' to clarify the sequence.

Think of a story you know well: a fairy tale, a movie plot, or a historical event. How would you retell it using the past perfect? For instance, "By the time Cinderella arrived at the ball, the prince had already danced with many guests." This structure sets the stage for the main action. It gives background information that helps the listener understand the context.

However, learners often confuse the past perfect with the simple past. The simple past tells us that something happened at a specific time in the past, while the past perfect tells us that something had happened before another past action. For example, "I finished my homework, and then I watched TV" (simple past, sequence) versus "I watched TV after I had finished my homework" (past perfect shows the earlier action).

Another common mistake is using past perfect too much. In English, we don''t need to use it for every action in a story; we only use it for the earlier action when we want to emphasize the order. For example, "When I got to the station, the train had left" is correct, but if we say "The train left at 9:00, and I arrived at 9:15," we use simple past for both because the order is clear.

Let''s practice with a story. Imagine you are telling a friend about a job interview you had yesterday. You might say, "I felt nervous because I hadn''t prepared enough." Here, ''hadn''t prepared'' shows that the lack of preparation happened before the interview. Or, "By the time the interviewer asked me a difficult question, I had already calmed down." This indicates that calming down happened before the question.

Now, think about a famous historical event: the sinking of the Titanic. You could say, "The Titanic had received several iceberg warnings before it hit the iceberg." This uses the past perfect to show that the warnings came first. Without it, we would have to say, "The Titanic received warnings, and then it hit the iceberg," which is less elegant.

In summary, the past perfect is a powerful tool for narrative control. It helps you set the scene, show cause and effect, and avoid ambiguity. As you practice, try to incorporate it into your own stories, whether you''re talking about your day, a film you''ve seen, or a news event. The more you use it, the more natural it will become.

Now, let''s test your understanding with a quiz. Each question will present a short narrative context, and you''ll need to choose the best sentence that uses the past perfect correctly to show the sequence of events. Remember to think about which action happened first.', 'Choose the best sentence that uses the past perfect to show the sequence of events.', '["When I arrived at the party, she left.","When I arrived at the party, she had left.","When I had arrived at the party, she left.","When I arrived at the party, she was leaving."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A forma correta é ''had left'' porque a ação de sair aconteceu antes da minha chegada. O passado perfeito indica a ação anterior.","options":["When I arrived at the party, she left.","When I arrived at the party, she had left.","When I had arrived at the party, she left.","When I arrived at the party, she was leaving."],"prompt":"Choose the best sentence that uses the past perfect to show the sequence of events."},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"A ordem correta é: primeiro terminar a lição (had finished) e depois assistir TV. A opção A usa o passado perfeito para a ação anterior.","options":["After I had finished my homework, I watched TV.","After I finished my homework, I had watched TV.","I had finished my homework, then I had watched TV.","I finished my homework after I had watched TV."],"prompt":"In which sentence is the past perfect used correctly?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A falta de preparação aconteceu antes do nervosismo, então usamos o passado perfeito ''hadn''t prepared''. A opção B está correta.","options":["I felt nervous because I didn''t prepare enough.","I felt nervous because I hadn''t prepared enough.","I had felt nervous because I didn''t prepare enough.","I had felt nervous because I hadn''t prepared enough."],"prompt":"Choose the sentence that correctly uses the past perfect to explain a situation."},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"A opção A usa ''had left'' para mostrar que a partida do trem foi anterior à minha chegada. As outras não usam o passado perfeito corretamente.","options":["The train had left before I arrived at the station.","The train left before I had arrived at the station.","The train has left before I arrived.","The train left before I arrived."],"prompt":"Which sentence uses the past perfect to show that an event happened before another?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O esquecimento do guarda-chuva aconteceu antes da chuva começar. Assim, ''had forgotten'' é o passado perfeito correto, e a opção B está certa.","options":["I forgot my umbrella after it had started to rain.","I had forgotten my umbrella when it started to rain.","I forgot my umbrella when it had started to rain.","I have forgotten my umbrella when it started to rain."],"prompt":"Choose the correct sentence to describe this scenario: You forgot to bring your umbrella, and then it started to rain."}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
on conflict (id) do update set title=excluded.title, body=excluded.body, question=excluded.question, options=excluded.options, answer_index=excluded.answer_index, questions=excluded.questions, is_published=excluded.is_published, updated_at=now();

insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b2-ffdfce760351', 'en', 'B2', 'The Hidden Story: Mastering the Advanced Passive Voice', 'When we read the news, listen to a lecture, or watch a documentary, we often encounter sentences like “The suspect was arrested last night” or “It has been announced that the new policy will take effect in March.” These sentences focus on the action and the receiver of the action, not on who performs it. This is the passive voice. At B2 level, you already know the basics, but there are more advanced uses that will make your English more natural and sophisticated.

Let''s start with why we use the passive. The passive is not just a grammatical exercise; it''s a tool for communication. We choose the passive when the doer (the agent) is unknown, obvious, or unimportant. For example, “My car was stolen” – we don''t know who stole it. “The rubbish is collected every Monday” – we don''t care who collects it; it''s a routine. Sometimes we use the passive to be formal or diplomatic, like “It was decided that the project would be postponed,” avoiding mentioning who decided. In other cases, we simply want to put the focus on the action or its result, not the doer.

Now, let''s look at the advanced forms. Beyond the simple present and past passive (is/was + past participle), you need to handle continuous, perfect, and modal passives, as well as passives with two objects and the impersonal passive.

**Continuous Passive:** “The road is being repaired.” (present continuous passive) or “The report was being typed when the power went out.” (past continuous passive). These emphasize that an action is or was in progress.

**Perfect Passive:** “The house has been sold.” (present perfect) or “By 2020, the old building had been demolished.” (past perfect). These link the action to a specific time or sequence.

**Modal Passive:** “The rules must be followed.” “The application can be submitted online.” “The meeting might be canceled.” Modals (can, should, must, might, etc.) combine with the passive to express necessity, possibility, etc.

**Passive with Two Objects:** When a verb has two objects (e.g., give, send, show), either object can become the subject. For example, in “Someone sent me a letter,” you can say “I was sent a letter” (using the person as subject) or “A letter was sent to me” (using the thing as subject). Both are correct, but the choice affects the focus.

**Impersonal Passive:** This is common in reporting and formal language: “It is said that...”, “It is believed that...”, “The film is thought to be a masterpiece.” We use “it” + passive verb + that clause. Also, with verbs of saying and thinking (think, believe, say, report), there''s a structure like “He is said to be very wealthy.” This is more advanced but very useful.

So, how do you form these? Let''s summarize:
- Present simple: am/is/are + past participle (e.g., “English is spoken worldwide.”)
- Past simple: was/were + past participle (e.g., “The letter was sent yesterday.”)
- Present continuous: am/is/are being + past participle (e.g., “The project is being reviewed.”)
- Past continuous: was/were being + past participle (e.g., “The match was being watched when I arrived.”)
- Present perfect: has/have been + past participle (e.g., “The results have been announced.”)
- Past perfect: had been + past participle (e.g., “The film had already started by the time we got there.”)
- Future: will be + past participle (e.g., “The issue will be addressed.”)
- Modals: modal + be + past participle (e.g., “The bill must be paid by tomorrow.”)
- Future perfect: will have been + past participle (e.g., “By next month, the renovation will have been completed.”)

Common mistakes to avoid: using the passive when the agent is important and active is better; using the passive with intransitive verbs (verbs that don''t take an object) like happen or exist; confusing the passive with the past tense; and using ''been'' incorrectly in continuous passives (don''t say "is being been").

Now, let''s practice. Here are five exercises to help you master these forms.

**Exercise 1: Present Continuous Passive**
Choose the correct form for the blank.

Example: The new bridge ________ at the moment.
Options: A) is building B) is being built C) has built D) built
Answer: B) is being built (Correct formation: present continuous passive is ''am/is/are being + past participle'')

Let''s proceed to the questions.', 'Which sentence correctly uses the present perfect passive?', '["The car has repaired already.","The car has been repaired already.","The car is been repaired already.","The car have been repaired already."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A forma correta do present perfect passive é ''has/have been + past participle''. Portanto, ''The car has been repaired'' está correto. As outras opções usam ''has repaired'' (ativo) ou ''is been'' (inventado).","options":["The car has repaired already.","The car has been repaired already.","The car is been repaired already.","The car have been repaired already."],"prompt":"Which sentence correctly uses the present perfect passive?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Com modais, a forma passiva é ''modal + be + past participle''. ''Must be done'' está correto. As outras opções omitem ''be'' ou usam ''do'' / ''to be done'' incorretamente.","options":["The homework must be done by Friday.","The homework must done by Friday.","The homework must be do by Friday.","The homework must to be done by Friday."],"prompt":"Choose the correct passive sentence with a modal verb."},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O passado contínuo passivo é ''was/were being + past participle''. ''was being delivered'' indica uma ação em andamento no passado. As outras opções usam simple past, present continuous, ou present perfect.","options":["The package was delivered yesterday.","The package was being delivered when I called.","The package is being delivered now.","The package has been delivered."],"prompt":"Which sentence is in the past continuous passive?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O futuro passivo é ''will be + past participle''. ''will be announced'' está correto. As outras opções omitem ''be'', usam a forma base, ou usam continuous.","options":["The results will be announced tomorrow.","The results will announced tomorrow.","The results will be announce tomorrow.","The results will be announcing tomorrow."],"prompt":"Rewrite the active sentence in the passive: ''They will announce the results tomorrow.'' Choose the correct passive form."},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O impessoal passivo usa ''it is + past participle + that clause''. ''It is said that...'' é um exemplo clássico. As outras opções são ativas ou passivas comuns.","options":["It is said that the company is planning a merger.","The company is planning a merger.","A merger is planned by the company.","The merger was planned."],"prompt":"Which sentence is an example of the impersonal passive?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
