insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b2-04e0404e4369', 'en', 'B2', 'Advanced Reported Speech: Shifting Perspectives', 'Reported speech, also known as indirect speech, is a way of telling someone what another person said without using their exact words. At the B2 level, you are probably familiar with the basic backshift of tenses (e.g., ''I am tired'' becomes ''She said she was tired''). However, advanced reported speech involves more subtle changes in pronouns, time expressions, and even modal verbs. It also includes reporting questions, commands, and requests in a natural way. Mastering these nuances will make your English sound more sophisticated and precise, especially in professional and academic settings.

One of the key challenges is the concept of ''backshift''. When the reporting verb is in the past (e.g., said, told), we often shift the tense of the original statement back one step. For example, ''I will help you'' becomes ''He said he would help me.'' But what if the original statement is still true? Many grammarians agree that you can keep the present tense if the situation hasn''t changed. For instance, ''The earth is round'' can be reported as ''The teacher said the earth is round'' (or ''was round''). Both are acceptable, but keeping the present tense emphasizes that it''s a general truth.

Another aspect is reporting questions. Unlike in direct speech, reported questions are not written with a question mark and follow the word order of a statement. For example, ''Where are you going?'' becomes ''She asked where I was going.'' Yes/no questions are introduced with ''if'' or ''whether'': ''Are you coming?'' becomes ''He asked if I was coming.'' It''s common to make mistakes here, such as inverting the subject and verb in the reported question, but remember: no inversion.

Commands and requests also have their own patterns. We use ''ask'' for requests and ''tell'' or ''order'' for commands, followed by the infinitive. For example, ''Please close the door'' becomes ''She asked me to close the door.'' To report a negative command, we use ''not to'': ''Don''t be late'' becomes ''He told me not to be late.'' This is a structure that many intermediate learners find tricky, but with practice it becomes second nature.

Modal verbs in reported speech can be particularly confusing. Some modals change (will → would, can → could), while others stay the same (might, could, would, should, ought to). For instance, ''I can help you'' becomes ''He said he could help me,'' but ''I might come'' becomes ''He said he might come'' (no change). The modal ''must'' can remain ''must'' or change to ''had to'' depending on whether it expresses obligation or a logical conclusion. For example, ''I must go now'' might become ''He said he had to go then,'' but ''This must be the right address'' can stay ''must'' as a deduction.

Finally, advanced reported speech often involves using reporting verbs other than ''said,'' such as ''admit,'' ''deny,'' ''insist,'' ''suggest,'' and ''explain.'' These verbs have different patterns: some take a that-clause (e.g., ''He admitted that he was wrong''), others take a gerund (e.g., ''She denied breaking the vase''), and still others take the subjunctive (e.g., ''I suggested that he see a doctor''). Using these verbs accurately shows a higher level of language proficiency. However, be careful with ''suggest'' – in formal English, it is followed by a that-clause with the base form of the verb, not ''to + infinitive.''

In summary, advanced reported speech requires careful attention to tense backshift, word order, pronoun changes, and the subtle differences among reporting verbs. Practice with real-life contexts will help you internalize these rules. Remember that context often determines whether the original tense should be kept or changed. As you become more confident, you''ll find yourself using reported speech naturally and flexibly.

Now, let''s test your understanding with a few questions. Read each situation carefully and choose the best reported sentence.', 'In reported speech, when do we often backshift the tense?', '["When the reporting verb is in the present tense","When the reporting verb is in the past tense","When the original statement is a general truth","When the reported statement is about the future"]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Normalmente, recuamos o tempo verbal (backshift) quando o verbo de reportagem está no passado, como ''said'' ou ''told''. Se está no presente, geralmente mantemos o tempo original.","options":["When the reporting verb is in the present tense","When the reporting verb is in the past tense","When the original statement is a general truth","When the reported statement is about the future"],"prompt":"In reported speech, when do we often backshift the tense?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Em perguntas indiretas, a ordem das palavras é de afirmação, e recuamos o tempo: ''where the station was''.","options":["She asked where the station was.","She asked where was the station.","She asked where is the station.","She asked where the station is."],"prompt":"Which of the following correctly reports the question ''Where is the station?''"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Para ordens negativas, usamos ''not to + verbo'' após o verbo de reportagem. A forma mais comum é ''ordered me not to touch''.","options":["He ordered me don''t touch that.","He ordered me not to touch that.","He ordered me to not touch that.","He ordered me that I don''t touch that."],"prompt":"How do we report the command ''Don''t touch that!''?"},{"answer_index":3,"explanation_pt_br":"O modal ''might'' não muda no discurso indireto, pois já é a forma passada de ''may''. Outros modais como ''will'' e ''can'' mudam para ''would'' e ''could''.","options":["will","can","may","might"],"prompt":"Which modal verb does NOT change when reported?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"''Insist'' pode ser seguido de ''on + gerúndio'' ou de ''that + sujeito + verbo'', mas também pode ser usado com ''to + infinitivo'' no sentido de ''insistir em fazer algo''. Ex.: ''He insisted to help us'' (embora menos comum, é gramatical).","options":["suggested","denied","insisted","explained"],"prompt":"Which reporting verb is correctly used in the sentence: ''He ___ to help us.''?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
