values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b2-15f78c78de97', 'en', 'B2', 'Connecting Ideas: Discourse Markers for Argumentative Writing', 'When you write an argumentative essay or engage in a debate, the way you connect your ideas is just as important as the ideas themselves. Discourse markers—words and phrases like ''however,'' ''therefore,'' and ''on the other hand''—are the glue that holds your argument together. They guide your reader or listener through your reasoning, signaling relationships between sentences and paragraphs. Without them, your writing can feel choppy and disjointed, even if your ideas are brilliant. In this lesson, we''ll explore how to use common discourse markers to express contrast, cause and effect, and addition, and we''ll see how they can make your arguments more persuasive and coherent.

Let''s start with markers of contrast. When you want to introduce a point that opposes what you''ve just said, you might use ''however,'' ''nevertheless,'' ''on the other hand,'' or ''in contrast.'' For example, ''Many people believe that technology isolates us. However, it also connects us in unprecedented ways.'' Notice how ''however'' signals a shift in perspective. Similarly, ''on the other hand'' often introduces an alternative viewpoint: ''Living in a big city has many advantages. On the other hand, it can be stressful and expensive.'' These markers help you acknowledge other sides of the issue, making your argument more balanced and nuanced.

Next, consider markers of cause and effect. Words like ''therefore,'' ''thus,'' ''consequently,'' and ''as a result'' show that one idea follows logically from another. For instance, ''The company invested heavily in renewable energy. As a result, its carbon footprint decreased significantly.'' Using these markers demonstrates that you can reason logically, which is a hallmark of strong argumentation. They help your reader follow your chain of reasoning, connecting causes to their effects clearly and concisely.

Another essential group is markers of addition, such as ''furthermore,'' ''moreover,'' ''in addition,'' and ''besides.'' These allow you to add supporting points or evidence to strengthen your case. For example, ''Regular exercise improves physical health. Furthermore, it boosts mental well-being and reduces stress.'' By using addition markers, you can build a cumulative case, showing that your argument has multiple layers of support.

However, it''s not enough to just scatter these markers throughout your text. You need to choose them carefully and place them where they make sense. Overusing them can make your writing seem mechanical, while using the wrong marker can confuse your reader. For example, using ''however'' when you want to add a point (''I like coffee. However, I also like tea.'') is not ideal; ''in addition'' or ''furthermore'' would be better. Similarly, ''therefore'' should only be used when there is a clear cause-and-effect relationship.

Let''s look at a sample paragraph that effectively uses these markers: ''Online learning offers flexibility for students who cannot attend traditional classes. However, it also demands high levels of self-discipline. On the one hand, students can study at their own pace and schedule. On the other hand, without face-to-face interaction, some may feel isolated. Therefore, educational institutions must provide adequate support systems, such as virtual office hours and discussion forums. Furthermore, they should train instructors in effective online teaching methods. In conclusion, while online learning presents challenges, it can be successful with the right strategies.''

As you can see, discourse markers are not just decorative; they are essential for clear communication. They signal to your audience how to interpret your ideas, whether you''re contrasting, adding, or concluding. By mastering these markers, you''ll be able to write more persuasive essays, present more compelling arguments, and participate more effectively in discussions.

Now, let''s practice what you''ve learned. The questions below will test your understanding of argumentative discourse markers and how to use them appropriately in context.', 'Which discourse marker best completes the sentence: ''The new policy has many benefits. ______, it simplifies the application process.''', '["However","Therefore","Furthermore","On the other hand"]'::jsonb, 2, '[{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"''Furthermore'' é usado para adicionar informação que apoia o ponto anterior, indicando ''além disso''. As outras opções indicam contraste ou consequência.","options":["However","Therefore","Furthermore","On the other hand"],"prompt":"Which discourse marker best completes the sentence: ''The new policy has many benefits. ______, it simplifies the application process.''"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''As a result'' indica consequência: trabalhar de casa resulta em economizar tempo de deslocamento. As outras opções não expressam causa e efeito.","options":["however","as a result","in contrast","also"],"prompt":"In the sentence, ''Many people prefer to work from home; ______, they save time on commuting,'' which marker shows cause and effect?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"''Nevertheless'' introduz contraste: apesar de ter estudado, ela falhou. ''Therefore'' e ''Moreover'' indicam causa e adição, respectivamente.","options":["Therefore","Moreover","Nevertheless","In addition"],"prompt":"Choose the correct marker to show contrast: ''She studied hard for the exam. ______, she failed.''"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''However'' é usado para introduzir uma ideia contrastante. A frase que apresenta uma oposição é a que diz que ele ama café, mas prefere chá.","options":["He loves coffee; however, he drinks it every morning.","He loves coffee; however, he prefers tea.","He loves coffee; however, he also loves tea.","He loves coffee; however, he drinks coffee."],"prompt":"Which sentence uses ''however'' correctly?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"A primeira lacuna pede contraste (porém) e a segunda consequência (portanto). A sequência ''However'' e ''Therefore'' atende a essa lógica.","options":["However; Therefore","Furthermore; However","In addition; Moreover","Thus; On the other hand"],"prompt":"Read the paragraph: ''Social media connects people globally. ______, it can also spread misinformation. ______, users must develop critical thinking skills.'' Which pair of markers completes the paragraph logically?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
