values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b2-45ec226401a7', 'en', 'B2', 'The Art of Collocation: Sounding Natural in English', 'When you reach an upper-intermediate level in English, you might notice that you can say almost anything you want, but sometimes your language sounds a bit... off. Native speakers might understand you perfectly, but something feels different. Often, this difference comes down to collocation – the way words naturally go together in English. For example, we say ''make a decision'', not ''do a decision'', and ''heavy rain'', not ''strong rain''. These combinations aren''t logical; they''re just how English works. Interestingly, your Portuguese intuition can sometimes lead you astray, especially with ''false friends'' – words that look similar but have different meanings. For instance, ''parents'' (pais) and ''parenti'' in Italian (relatives) are a classic example, but Portuguese has its own traps like ''pretend'' (fingir) vs. ''pretender'' (to intend). To sound natural, you need to train your ear and eye to notice these patterns. So, what can you do? First, read extensively in English – news articles, blogs, even social media. Pay attention to how verbs and nouns combine. Second, use a learner''s dictionary to check common collocations. Third, keep a notebook of new combinations you encounter. Also, consider the ''immersive method'': don''t just study English, live it. Watch your favorite series in English (start with Portuguese subtitles, then switch to English), set your phone to English, and take notes in English. These activities expose you to language in context, which is how we acquire collocations naturally. But be careful: trying to translate word-for-word is a recipe for miscommunication, especially in fast conversations. Instead, focus on key words – usually nouns and verbs – to get the gist. Over time, you''ll develop a feel for what sounds right. Now, collocations are not the only challenge. English also has many idioms – expressions whose meanings aren''t predictable from the individual words. For example, ''to be on the ball'' means to be quick to understand, and ''to hit the sack'' means to go to bed. Using idioms can make your speech more colorful, but they can also confuse if used inappropriately. At B2 level, you should be able to understand common idioms and use them in informal contexts. Think about register, too: idioms are usually informal, so you wouldn''t use them in a formal report. Similarly, some collocations are more formal or technical. For instance, ''conduct an investigation'' is more formal than ''carry out an investigation''. As you progress, you''ll learn to switch between registers naturally. This skill is crucial for the Cambridge FCE exam, where you need to demonstrate a range of vocabulary. To improve, practice paraphrasing: take a sentence and express it in different ways, using synonyms and alternative collocations. This will help you avoid repetition and sound more sophisticated. In the end, language learning is a long journey, but with consistent effort, you''ll find that English becomes second nature. So, immerse yourself, be curious, and don''t be afraid to make mistakes – they''re part of the process.', 'What is the main idea of the text?', '["Collocations are the most difficult part of English","To sound natural in English, you need to learn collocations and idioms, and be mindful of register","You should translate word-for-word to learn English faster","Portuguese speakers have an advantage because of false friends"]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto aborda a importância das colocações (collocations) e expressões idiomáticas para soar natural, além de mencionar o registro. As outras opções são incorretas ou exageradas.","options":["Collocations are the most difficult part of English","To sound natural in English, you need to learn collocations and idioms, and be mindful of register","You should translate word-for-word to learn English faster","Portuguese speakers have an advantage because of false friends"],"prompt":"What is the main idea of the text?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto explica que traduzir palavra por palavra é difícil e pode levar a mal-entendidos, especialmente com falsos cognatos. Além disso, não é eficiente para acompanhar conversas.","options":["Because it''s too slow and you can easily fall into false friends","Because English has more words than Portuguese","Because it''s against the immersive method","Because it''s only useful for beginners"],"prompt":"Why is it not advisable to translate words one by one into English?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto cita ''make a decision'' como exemplo correto, enquanto ''do a decision'' é incorreto. ''Strong rain'' não é mencionado; o texto diz ''heavy rain''. ''Parents e parenti'' é um exemplo de falsos cognatos.","options":["make a decision","do a decision","strong rain","parents and parenti"],"prompt":"Which of the following is an example of a collocation mentioned in the text?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto descreve o método imersivo como ''não estude o idioma, viva-o'', incluindo assistir séries, mudar o celular para inglês, etc. As outras opções não correspondem.","options":["Studying grammar rules intensively","Living the language by incorporating it into your daily life","Listening to music only","Taking formal classes"],"prompt":"What does the immersive method involve according to the text?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto menciona ''pretend'' como um falso cognato, pois em inglês significa ''fingir'', mas em português ''pretender'' significa ''to intend''. As outras opções são cognatos verdadeiros ou não mencionados.","options":["pretend (fingir)","parents (pais)","heavy (pesado)","decision (decisão)"],"prompt":"Which of the following is a false friend for Portuguese speakers?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
