values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b2-d08b0024d2d1', 'en', 'B2', 'The Advanced Passive: Mastering Nuance in Formal English', 'The passive voice is a crucial tool in English, especially in formal and academic contexts. While at B1 level you learn the basics—like ''The letter was written''—at B2, you need to master more advanced forms and uses. This lesson will guide you through the advanced passive, helping you sound more natural and precise. We''ll explore when to use it, how to form it in various tenses, and how to avoid common mistakes. By the end, you''ll be able to use the passive with confidence in both writing and speaking.

First, let''s understand why we use the passive. We often choose the passive when the action is more important than the doer, or when the doer is unknown or obvious. For example, ''My car was stolen last night.'' We don''t know who stole it, so the passive is perfect. We also use it to be impersonal or formal, like in scientific reports: ''The experiment was conducted under controlled conditions.'' In news reports, the passive is everywhere: ''A new policy has been announced by the government.'' It shifts the focus from the agent to the action or the receiver.

Now, let''s look at forming the advanced passive. The basic structure is: subject + form of ''to be'' + past participle. But what about different tenses? You need to be comfortable with passives in various tenses. For example, present continuous: ''The road is being repaired.'' Past continuous: ''The bridge was being painted when I arrived.'' Present perfect: ''The report has been finished.'' Past perfect: ''The project had been completed before the deadline.'' Future: ''The decision will be made tomorrow.'' And with modals: ''The rules must be followed.'' Each of these uses a specific form of ''to be'' plus the past participle. Notice how the continuous forms use ''being''.

A particularly advanced structure is the passive with two objects. Some verbs like ''give'', ''send'', ''show'', and ''tell'' can have two objects: a direct and an indirect. In the active voice, you can say ''Someone gave me a gift'' or ''Someone gave a gift to me.'' In the passive, you can make either object the subject. For example: ''I was given a gift'' or ''A gift was given to me.'' The first is more common in spoken English because we usually start with the person. Similarly, ''They told us a story'' becomes ''We were told a story'' or ''A story was told to us.''

Another advanced use is the passive infinitive and -ing form. For example: ''The report needs to be checked.'' Here, ''to be checked'' is a passive infinitive. Or ''I remember being taken to the zoo as a child.'' ''Being taken'' is a passive -ing form. These structures allow you to combine the passive with other verbs smoothly. Also, note the use of ''get'' as an informal passive: ''He got fired from his job.'' This is common in spoken English, though ''was fired'' is more formal.

Now, let''s think about when to avoid the passive. Overusing it can make your writing sound wordy and impersonal. In general, prefer the active voice when you want to be direct and dynamic. For instance, compare ''The company made a mistake'' (active) and ''A mistake was made by the company'' (passive). The first is clearer and more concise. The passive is especially useful in scientific and news writing, but in everyday conversation, we often favor the active. Also, be careful with the ''by'' phrase. You don''t always need it: ''The cake was eaten'' is fine without mentioning who ate it. Only include ''by'' if the agent is important or surprising.

Finally, let''s address common mistakes. One frequent error is forgetting to change the verb form correctly. For example, saying ''The report was wrote'' instead of ''The report was written.'' Another is using the passive with intransitive verbs that don''t take an object: you can''t say ''The accident was happened'' because ''happen'' has no object. Also, some learners confuse the passive with the past participle used as an adjective. For example, ''The window is broken'' describes a state, not an action. To avoid these, always check that your verb has a direct object in the active form, and use the correct past participle. With practice, you''ll be able to choose between active and passive naturally, enhancing your English fluency.

In summary, the advanced passive is a versatile structure that can make your English more sophisticated. By mastering its forms, uses, and nuances, you''ll be better equipped for academic and professional settings. Remember to vary your sentence structures and choose the voice that best fits your message. Now, let''s test your understanding with some questions.', 'Why does the author suggest using the passive voice in scientific reports?', '["To make the text longer","To appear more objective and formal","To emphasize the researcher''s role","To avoid using past participles"]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O autor afirma que o passivo é usado para ser impessoal e formal, como em relatórios científicos, deslocando o foco da ação e não do agente.","options":["To make the text longer","To appear more objective and formal","To emphasize the researcher''s role","To avoid using past participles"],"prompt":"Why does the author suggest using the passive voice in scientific reports?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O presente contínuo passivo é formado com ''am/is/are being + past participle''. ''The road is being repaired'' está correto, indicando ação em andamento.","options":["The report is finished.","The road is being repaired.","The bridge was painted.","The decision has been made."],"prompt":"Which sentence correctly uses the passive voice in the present continuous tense?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Na ativa, ''Someone gave me a gift'' tem objeto direto ''a gift''. No passivo, o objeto indireto ''me'' tornou-se sujeito, e o direto permanece.","options":["I","a gift","was given","nothing"],"prompt":"In the passive sentence ''I was given a gift'', what was the direct object in the active sentence?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O infinitivo passivo é ''to be + past participle''. ''To be checked'' é a forma correta, enquanto as outras não são passivas infinitivas.","options":["The report needs to be checked.","The report needs to check.","The report is checked.","The report has been checked."],"prompt":"Which sentence uses a passive infinitive correctly?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto menciona que um erro comum é usar passivo com verbos intransitivos, como ''The accident was happened'', pois ''happen'' não tem objeto.","options":["Using ''get'' instead of ''be'' in passives.","Using the passive for intransitive verbs.","Including the ''by'' phrase when unnecessary.","Using the active voice too often."],"prompt":"What is a common mistake mentioned in the passage?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
