values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b2-78fdde210305', 'en', 'B2', 'Advanced Reported Speech: Shifting Perspectives in English', 'When we tell someone what another person said, we often need to change the original words to fit our new context. This is called reported speech, and at the B2 level, it goes beyond simple tense shifts. You need to manage time references, modal verbs, and even the meaning itself. Consider this example: ''I will help you tomorrow,'' she said. If you report this on the same day, you might say, ''She said she will help me tomorrow.'' But if you report it the next day, it becomes ''She said she would help me the next day.'' The change from ''will'' to ''would'' and from ''tomorrow'' to ''the next day'' reflects the shift in time perspective.

One common challenge is reporting questions. In direct speech, we ask, ''Where is the station?'' In reported speech, this becomes ''She asked where the station was.'' Notice the word order changes to that of a statement, and the tense often moves back. Similarly, commands and requests are reported using infinitives: ''Please close the door'' becomes ''He asked me to close the door.'' It''s not just about changing tenses; it''s about conveying the original intent accurately.

Modal verbs often change in reported speech. For instance, ''can'' usually becomes ''could,'' and ''may'' often changes to ''might.'' However, ''should'' and ''would'' typically stay the same. These nuances are crucial for expressing degrees of certainty or possibility. For example, ''I can swim'' reported becomes ''He said he could swim,'' but ''I should go'' remains ''He said he should go.'' Understanding these shifts helps you maintain the original meaning.

Another tricky aspect is reporting statements about facts or truths that are still true. In such cases, you can sometimes keep the original tense. For instance, ''The Earth orbits the Sun'' can be reported as ''The teacher said the Earth orbits the Sun'' or ''...orbited the Sun.'' Both are acceptable, but the present tense emphasizes that it''s a general truth.

Finally, practice is key. Try reporting conversations you hear in movies or podcasts. Pay attention to how pronouns, time expressions, and tenses change. For example, note how ''I'' becomes ''he'' or ''she,'' and ''here'' becomes ''there.'' These details make your English sound natural and precise.

Now, let''s check your understanding with these exercises.', 'Choose the correct reported speech for the direct sentence: ''I am feeling sick,'' she said.', '["She said she was feeling sick.","She said she is feeling sick.","She said I was feeling sick.","She said she felt sick."]'::jsonb, 0, '[{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"A forma correta é ''She said she was feeling sick'' porque o presente contínuo ''am feeling'' vira passado contínuo ''was feeling'' no discurso indireto. As outras opções têm erros de pronome ou tempo.","options":["She said she was feeling sick.","She said she is feeling sick.","She said I was feeling sick.","She said she felt sick."],"prompt":"Choose the correct reported speech for the direct sentence: ''I am feeling sick,'' she said."},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"No discurso indireto, perguntas sim/não usam ''if'' ou ''whether'' e o verbo volta ao passado. ''Liked'' está correto. A ordem das palavras deve ser de afirmação.","options":["He asked if I like coffee.","He asked if I liked coffee.","He asked did I like coffee.","He asked whether I like coffee."],"prompt":"Report the question: ''Do you like coffee?'' he asked."},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O modal ''can'' geralmente muda para ''could'' no discurso indireto. ''Could'' indica habilidade no passado e mantém o significado.","options":["He said he can help me.","He said he could help me.","He said I could help him.","He said he would help me."],"prompt":"How would you report: ''I can help you,'' he said?"},{"answer_index":3,"explanation_pt_br":"Para ordens ou pedidos, usamos ''asked + pessoa + to + verbo''. ''Asked us'' é a forma correta. ''Said'' não é usado com infinitivo.","options":["The teacher said to be quiet.","The teacher said us to be quiet.","The teacher asked to be quiet.","The teacher asked us to be quiet."],"prompt":"Report the imperative: ''Please be quiet,'' the teacher said."},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Quando no dia seguinte, ''tomorrow'' vira ''the next day'' e ''will'' vira ''would''. A opção que reflete ambos os ajustes é a correta.","options":["John said he would come tomorrow.","John said he will come the next day.","John said he would come the next day.","John said he will come tomorrow."],"prompt":"If today is Monday and John said ''I will come tomorrow'', how would you report it on Tuesday?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
