values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b2-8886371f2919', 'en', 'B2', 'Linking Words for Strong Arguments', 'Have you ever found yourself in a discussion where you know what you want to say but struggle to connect your ideas smoothly? Argumentative discourse markers are like the scaffolding of a well-built argument. They help you structure your thoughts, show relationships between ideas, and guide your listener or reader through your reasoning. Without them, even the most brilliant points can come across as a jumble of disconnected statements.

In English, these markers are often adverbs or short phrases that signal a shift in the argument. For instance, ''furthermore'' adds a supporting point, while ''nevertheless'' introduces a contrasting idea. They act as signposts, telling the audience whether to expect more evidence, a counterargument, or a conclusion. Mastering them is essential for B2-level communication, whether you''re writing an essay, participating in a debate, or simply having a lively conversation with friends.

Consider a typical debate: you might start with ''To begin with,'' then present your main points using ''in addition'' and ''moreover.'' If someone raises an objection, you could respond with ''however'' or ''on the other hand.'' When you want to concede a point but still stand your ground, ''even so'' or ''nonetheless'' are perfect. Finally, to wrap up, you''d use ''in conclusion'' or ''to sum up.'' Each marker serves a specific purpose, and using them correctly makes you sound more persuasive and fluent.

One common pitfall is overusing the same markers, which makes your speech or writing repetitive. Instead of relying solely on ''but'' and ''so,'' broaden your repertoire. Try ''whereas'' to contrast, ''therefore'' to show consequence, and ''as a result'' to indicate cause and effect. Not only do these add variety, but they also make your arguments more nuanced. Another tip: pay attention to the position of these markers. While some can appear at the beginning of a sentence, others fit better in the middle, after a semicolon or comma. For example, ''however'' can start a sentence or follow a semicolon: ''The plan was risky; however, we decided to go ahead.''

In academic writing, argumentative markers are crucial for coherence. When you write an essay, you need to link paragraphs and sentences logically. Transition words like ''firstly,'' ''secondly,'' and ''finally'' organize your points. Others like ''as a result'' and ''consequently'' show cause and effect, which is often needed in argumentative essays that rely on evidence. Without these, your writing appears choppy and hard to follow. The key is to use them intentionally, not just as fillers, but to actually clarify the relationships between your ideas.

For Brazilian learners, there''s a tendency to transfer Portuguese discourse markers directly into English. For example, ''por outro lado'' is not ''on the other side,'' but ''on the other hand.'' Similarly, ''além disso'' is ''furthermore'' or ''in addition,'' not ''besides that.'' Recognizing these false friends will help you avoid common mistakes. It''s also important to note that English often uses a comma after the marker at the beginning of a sentence, while in Portuguese the rules may differ. Paying close attention to these details will refine your English style.

In a digital age, where we often communicate in writing through emails and social media, argumentative markers are just as important. They help convey tone and intent, preventing misunderstandings. For instance, using ''despite'' instead of ''in spite of'' might confuse some readers, but the meaning is the same. Being aware of register is also key: some markers are formal (''moreover''), others neutral (''also''), and some informal (''plus''). Choose accordingly. By mastering these discourse markers, you''ll not only sound more proficient but also more confident in expressing your ideas. So next time you argue, remember to use them wisely.

Source: Writing and speaking skills for B2 learners, adapted from various educational resources.', 'What is the primary function of argumentative discourse markers?', '["To make sentences longer","To connect ideas and show relationships","To replace nouns and verbs","To confuse the reader"]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Marcadores discursivos argumentativos são usados para conectar ideias, mostrar relações lógicas e guiar o interlocutor ou leitor através do raciocínio.","options":["To make sentences longer","To connect ideas and show relationships","To replace nouns and verbs","To confuse the reader"],"prompt":"What is the primary function of argumentative discourse markers?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"''Nevertheless'' indica contraste, equivalente a ''no entanto'' ou ''apesar disso''. Os outros marcadores adicionam informações.","options":["furthermore","moreover","nevertheless","in addition"],"prompt":"Which marker would you use to introduce a contrasting point?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''However'' é usado para introduzir um contraste, e pode aparecer após ponto e vírgula. A frase expressa uma oposição entre o risco e a decisão.","options":["moreover","however","therefore","besides"],"prompt":"In the sentence ''The plan was risky; ______, we decided to go ahead,'' which marker fits best?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Muitos brasileiros traduzem ''por outro lado'' literalmente como ''on the other side'', mas o correto é ''on the other hand''.","options":["Using ''on the other hand'' correctly","Saying ''on the other side'' for ''por outro lado''","Using ''furthermore'' in formal writing","Placing a comma after ''however''"],"prompt":"Which of the following is a common mistake for Brazilian learners?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''Moreover'' é um marcador formal, usado em textos acadêmicos para acrescentar argumentos. Os outros são mais informais.","options":["plus","moreover","so","but"],"prompt":"Which marker is more appropriate for a formal essay?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
