values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b2-c861da76ad40', 'en', 'B2', 'Idioms and Register in English', 'When you reach an upper-intermediate level in English, you might notice that native speakers often use expressions that don''t make sense if you translate them word-for-word. These are called idioms. For example, if someone says ''It''s raining cats and dogs'', they don''t expect animals to fall from the sky; they simply mean it''s raining heavily. Idioms add color to the language, but they also require you to understand the context and the register (the level of formality) in which they are used.

Consider the difference between formal and informal registers. In a job interview, you would probably say, ''I believe I am well-qualified for this position'' rather than ''I''m a great fit for this gig.'' The latter would sound too casual and might give the wrong impression. Similarly, in academic writing, you would avoid idioms or colloquial expressions, as they may be seen as too informal. Instead, you would choose more precise and formal vocabulary.

Idioms often belong to specific registers. For instance, ''to kick the bucket'' is a very informal (and somewhat disrespectful) way to say ''to die.'' You would use it in a casual conversation among friends, but never in a eulogy or a sympathy card. On the other hand, ''to pass away'' is neutral and respectful, suitable for formal contexts. Understanding these nuances is crucial for effective communication, especially if you plan to work or study in an English-speaking environment.

Another important aspect is the use of phrasal verbs, which are common in informal English. For example, ''to look into'' is a phrasal verb meaning ''to investigate.'' In formal writing, you might prefer ''to investigate'' instead. However, in everyday conversation, phrasal verbs are natural and expected. Knowing when to use them can make your speech sound more native-like.

One common mistake learners make is translating idioms from their native language directly into English. This often results in expressions that sound confusing or nonsensical. For instance, a Portuguese speaker might say ''give a banana'' (meaning to make a mistake) but in English, we say ''make a mistake'' or ''mess up.'' To avoid this, it''s helpful to learn idioms in context and practice using them appropriately.

Finally, remember that idioms are not the only aspect of register. The vocabulary you choose, the structure of your sentences, and even your pronunciation can indicate formality. For example, using contractions (like ''don''t'' instead of ''do not'') makes your speech less formal. Paying attention to these details will help you switch between registers with confidence.

In summary, mastering idioms and register is a key step in becoming fluent in English. It allows you to express yourself more precisely and to adapt your language to different social and professional situations. So, don''t be afraid to explore idioms, but always be mindful of the context and the people you are speaking to. With practice, you''ll be able to use them naturally, just like a native speaker.

Now, test your understanding with the following questions.', 'What does the idiom ''It''s raining cats and dogs'' mean?', '["It''s raining lightly.","Animals are falling from the sky.","It''s raining heavily.","It''s very cold."]'::jsonb, 2, '[{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"A expressão ''It''s raining cats and dogs'' é um idioma que significa ''chovendo muito'' ou ''chovendo canivetes''. Não tem relação literal com animais. As outras opções não correspondem ao significado idiomático.","options":["It''s raining lightly.","Animals are falling from the sky.","It''s raining heavily.","It''s very cold."],"prompt":"What does the idiom ''It''s raining cats and dogs'' mean?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A expressão ''to kick the bucket'' é muito informal e até um pouco insensível. Sendo assim, é adequada apenas em contextos informais, como uma conversa entre amigos. Nas outras opções, seria inadequada por ser formal ou solene demais.","options":["In a formal business meeting.","In a conversation with close friends about a deceased pet.","In an academic essay about mortality.","In a sympathy card to a colleague."],"prompt":"In which of the following situations would it be most appropriate to use the phrase ''to kick the bucket''?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Em uma entrevista de emprego formal, é importante usar linguagem profissional e educada. A opção 2 é a mais formal e adequada. As outras opções são coloquiais ou informais demais (gig, super good, stuff, you know) e podem transmitir uma imagem inadequada.","options":["I''m a great fit for this gig.","I believe I am well-qualified for this position.","I''m super good at this stuff.","I''ve got the skills, you know?"],"prompt":"Which phrase is more suitable for a formal job interview?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O phrasal verb ''look into'' é mais comum em contextos informais e significa ''investigar''. As outras opções (''investigate'', ''examine'', ''analyze'') são verbos mais formais, usados em contextos acadêmicos ou profissionais.","options":["Investigate","Look into","Examine","Analyze"],"prompt":"Which of the following is a phrasal verb commonly used in informal English?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A expressão ''give a banana'' é uma tradução literal de uma expressão em português, mas não existe em inglês. Para dizer ''cometer um erro'', usamos ''make a mistake'' ou ''mess up''. Idiomas não podem ser traduzidos palavra por palavra.","options":["It is too formal.","It is not an English idiom; the correct English idiom is ''to make a mistake''.","It is offensive.","It is an idiom, but it''s outdated."],"prompt":"A learner says ''give a banana'' meaning ''to make a mistake''. Why is this incorrect?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
