values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b2-bbc5c42772c0', 'en', 'B2', 'The Art of Being Understood: Advanced Passive Structures', 'Have you ever noticed how news reports often say ''The decision was made'' instead of ''They made the decision''? This shift in focus, from who did the action to the action itself, is the essence of the passive voice. At B2 level, you already know the basic passive (be + past participle), but English offers more sophisticated passive structures that can make your speech and writing more natural and precise. Let''s explore these advanced forms and see how they are used in real-life contexts.

The passive voice is particularly common in academic writing, journalism, and formal reports. For example, a scientist might write, ''The experiment was conducted over a period of six months.'' Here, the focus is on the experiment, not on the researcher. This is especially useful when the agent (the person or thing doing the action) is unknown, obvious, or unimportant. For instance, ''My car was stolen last night'' – we don''t know who stole it, and it''s not important. Similarly, ''The rubbish is collected every Monday'' – everyone knows it''s the garbage collectors, so we don''t need to mention them.

One advanced form is the passive with ''get''. While ''be'' is more formal, ''get'' is common in informal speech. For example, ''He got fired from his job'' is more colloquial than ''He was fired.'' ''Get'' often implies a change of state or an unexpected event, as in ''I got caught in the rain.'' This structure is very common in everyday English, and mastering it will make you sound more fluent and natural.

Another sophisticated structure is the passive with ''have'' or ''get'' something done. This is used when we arrange for someone else to do something for us. For example, ''I had my hair cut yesterday'' or ''We''re getting the house painted next week.'' This is a very practical construction, especially when talking about services. A Brazilian learner might be tempted to say ''I cut my hair'' (which would mean they did it themselves), but in English, ''I had my hair cut'' is the norm.

Finally, we have the impersonal passive, which is often used in formal and academic language. Instead of saying ''People say that...'', we can say ''It is said that...'' or ''He is said to be...''. This structure allows us to report opinions and beliefs without specifying the source. For example, ''It is believed that the company will go bankrupt'' or ''The new policy is thought to be unpopular.'' This is a powerful tool for sounding objective and detached in discussions.

Advanced passive structures also include the passive infinitive (e.g., ''The report needs to be finished by Friday'') and the passive -ing form (e.g., ''She enjoys being photographed''). These are used after certain verbs and prepositions. For example, ''The house needs to be cleaned'' or ''He is interested in being promoted.'' These forms are essential for expressing necessity, desire, or opinion in a passive way.

It''s important to note common mistakes with advanced passives. One frequent error is using the wrong tense of ''be'' or ''get'', especially when the action happens in the past. For example, ''The problem was solved'' not ''The problem is solved'' if it happened yesterday. Another mistake is confusing the passive with the active in complex sentences, such as ''The manager was explained the problem'' – the correct form is ''The problem was explained to the manager.''

To master advanced passives, practice is key. Try to notice them in your reading: how do news articles report events? How do academics express findings? When you listen to English podcasts or watch series, pay attention to how ''get'' and ''have something done'' are used. Then, try to incorporate these structures into your own speaking and writing. For example, instead of saying ''They are building a new bridge'', you could say ''A new bridge is being built'' or ''They are having a new bridge built.'' These nuances will elevate your English to a more sophisticated level.

In conclusion, advanced passive structures are not just grammar rules to memorize; they are tools for effective communication. By using them correctly, you can shift the focus of a sentence, avoid mentioning unimportant agents, and sound more formal or natural depending on the context. As you continue your English journey, remember that these structures are widely used in everyday language, so making them part of your active vocabulary is essential. Now, let''s test your understanding with some practice questions.', 'In which situation is the passive voice most appropriate?', '["When you want to emphasize the person who performed the action.","When the agent is unknown, obvious, or unimportant.","When you are writing a personal email to a friend.","When you want to show your own responsibility for an action."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A voz passiva é usada quando o agente (quem faz a ação) é desconhecido, óbvio ou irrelevante. Por exemplo, em ''My car was stolen'', não sabemos quem roubou, e em ''The rubbish is collected'', todos sabem que são os lixeiros.","options":["When you want to emphasize the person who performed the action.","When the agent is unknown, obvious, or unimportant.","When you are writing a personal email to a friend.","When you want to show your own responsibility for an action."],"prompt":"In which situation is the passive voice most appropriate?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A estrutura ''get something done'' indica que alguém fez algo por você. ''She got her nails done'' significa que ela foi ao salão fazer as unhas. As outras opções usam ''get'' com o sentido de ''ser'' que não é natural nesses exemplos.","options":["The letter got written by Mary.","She got her nails done for the party.","The book got published by a famous company.","He got given a present by his parents."],"prompt":"Which sentence uses the ''get'' passive in a natural way?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A estrutura ''have something done'' significa que você arranja para que outra pessoa faça algo. Portanto, ''I had my bike repaired'' indica que você pagou a alguém para consertar a bicicleta.","options":["I repaired my bike myself.","I paid someone to repair my bike.","I watched someone repair my bike.","I plan to repair my bike."],"prompt":"What does ''I had my bike repaired'' mean?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O passivo impessoal usa ''It is said/reported/believed that...'' para relatar opiniões ou informações sem mencionar a fonte. A opção correta é ''It is said that he is very rich''. As outras são passivos comuns.","options":["The cake was baked by my mother.","It is said that he is very rich.","The house was built in 1990.","The work was done quickly."],"prompt":"Which sentence is an example of impersonal passive?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Após ''needs'', usamos o infinitivo passivo: ''needs to be finished''. As outras opções têm formas incorretas.","options":["needs to be finished","needs to finish","needs being finished","needs to been finished"],"prompt":"Choose the correct passive form: ''The report ______ by Friday.''"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
