insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b2-a1f231895720', 'en', 'B2', 'Advanced Reported Speech: Mastering Indirect Communication', 'Reported speech, also known as indirect speech, is a vital skill for upper-intermediate English learners. It allows you to relay what someone else has said without using their exact words, which is essential in academic writing, professional communication, and everyday conversation. At B2 level, you are expected to move beyond simple tense backshift and handle more complex structures, such as reporting questions, commands, and using advanced reporting verbs. This lesson will guide you through the intricacies of reported speech, focusing on precision and natural usage.

When we report speech, we often need to change the tense of the original statement. For example, if someone says, "I am tired," you might report, "She said she was tired." This is called backshifting. However, the rules are not always strict. If the reporting verb is in the present (e.g., "she says"), we do not backshift. Also, if the original statement is a general truth or still true, you can keep the original tense. For instance, "He said that the Earth orbits the Sun." This flexibility is a hallmark of advanced usage.

Reporting questions requires careful attention to word order. In indirect questions, we use normal statement word order, not the inverted order of direct questions. For example, "Where is the station?" becomes "She asked where the station was." We also omit the question mark. For yes/no questions, we use ''if'' or ''whether'': "He asked if I was coming."

Commands and requests are typically reported using ''tell'', ''ask'', or ''order'' followed by an infinitive. For example, "Please close the door" becomes "She asked me to close the door." Negative commands use ''not to'' + base verb: "He told me not to be late." Using a variety of reporting verbs can make your speech more precise. Instead of always using ''said'', consider verbs like ''admit'', ''deny'', ''insist'', ''suggest'', ''explain'', or ''warn''. Each verb has a specific pattern: ''suggest + gerund'' (suggest going), ''insist on + gerund'' (insist on staying), or ''warn somebody not to'' (warned me not to).

Another advanced aspect is reporting thoughts and beliefs. You can use structures like ''He thought that...'' or ''She believed that...''. With these, backshifting is common but not obligatory if the situation is current. Additionally, when reporting immediate speech, you might use ''say'' with a direct object: "He said to me, ''I''ll call you.''" becomes "He told me he would call me." Notice the change from ''said to'' to ''told''.

Common mistakes at this level include forgetting to change time and place expressions. For instance, ''today'' becomes ''that day'', ''yesterday'' becomes ''the day before'', and ''here'' becomes ''there''. Also, many learners forget to backshift modals correctly: ''will'' becomes ''would'', ''can'' becomes ''could'', but ''must'' can stay ''must'' or change to ''had to''. Practice these transformations to avoid errors.

Now, let''s test your understanding with some exercises. Remember to focus on the context and the tense changes.', 'Choose the correct reported statement: Direct speech: "I am learning Spanish," said Maria.', '["Maria said she is learning Spanish.","Maria said she was learning Spanish.","Maria said I was learning Spanish.","Maria said she has been learning Spanish."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Quando o verbo de reportagem está no passado (said), geralmente recuamos o tempo do discurso direto. ''am'' (presente contínuo) vira ''was'' (passado contínuo).","options":["Maria said she is learning Spanish.","Maria said she was learning Spanish.","Maria said I was learning Spanish.","Maria said she has been learning Spanish."],"prompt":"Choose the correct reported statement: Direct speech: \"I am learning Spanish,\" said Maria."},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Em perguntas indiretas, usamos a ordem normal da frase (sujeito + verbo) e recuamos o tempo. ''do you live'' vira ''I lived''.","options":["She asked me where did I live.","She asked me where I lived.","She asked me where do I live.","She asked me where I live."],"prompt":"Which sentence correctly reports the question: \"Where do you live?\""},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Comandos negativos são geralmente relatados com ''told + pessoa + not to + verbo''. ''He told me not to touch that'' é a forma correta.","options":["He said me not to touch that.","He said to me not to touch that.","He told me not to touch that.","He told me to not touch that."],"prompt":"Choose the best reported command: \"Don''t touch that!\""},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Com verbo de reportagem no passado, ''will'' vira ''would'' e ''tomorrow'' vira ''the next day'' ou ''the following day''.","options":["He said he will help me tomorrow.","He said he would help me the next day.","He said he would help me tomorrow.","He said he will help me the next day."],"prompt":"Complete with the correct reported speech: Direct: \"I will help you tomorrow,\" he said."},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O verbo ''deny'' é seguido de gerúndio (-ing). ''Denied taking'' é a forma correta. Outros verbos com padrões específicos incluem ''suggest + ing'' e ''warn + to''.","options":["He denied to take the money.","He denied taking the money.","He denied to have taken the money.","He denied that he didn''t take the money."],"prompt":"Which sentence correctly reports the statement using an advanced verb: \"I didn''t take the money,\" he said."}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
