values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b2-13f339d982e5', 'en', 'B2', 'The Advanced Passive: From Rules to Nuance', 'The passive voice is a cornerstone of English grammar, and at the B2 level, you are expected to move beyond the simple ''is done'' and ''was done'' forms. This lesson will guide you through the advanced passive structures that will make your English more sophisticated and natural. You''ll learn how to use the passive in different tenses, with modal verbs, and in more complex constructions like the impersonal passive and the causative. By the end, you''ll be able to understand and use these forms confidently in both writing and speaking.

Why is the passive so important? In academic writing, reports, and news, the passive is used to focus on the action rather than the person doing it. For example, ''The new policy was announced yesterday'' is more formal and objective than ''They announced the new policy yesterday.'' In everyday conversation, you might use the passive when you don''t know who did something or when it''s obvious. For instance, ''My car was stolen'' focuses on the car, not the thief. This ability to shift the focus is crucial for effective communication.

Now, let''s dive into the advanced forms. First, we have the passive with modal verbs: ''The report must be submitted by Friday.'' Here, ''must'' indicates obligation, and the passive form ''be submitted'' shows that the report is the receiver of the action. Other modals like ''should'', ''can'', ''might'', and ''could'' are used similarly. For example, ''This problem can be solved easily.''

Second, there''s the continuous passive, which describes an ongoing action: ''The new bridge is being built.'' The structure is ''is/am/are being + past participle.'' For past continuous, you''d say ''The house was being painted when I arrived.'' This tense is often used to set the scene or emphasize that an action was in progress.

Third, the perfect passive is formed with ''have/has been + past participle.'' For example, ''The decision has been made.'' This form connects the past action to the present. The past perfect passive, ''had been done'', is used in reported speech or to show that one action happened before another in the past.

Finally, there are two advanced constructions that are particularly useful: the impersonal passive and the causative. The impersonal passive uses ''It is said/thought/believed that...'' to report general opinions. For instance, ''It is believed that the company will merge.'' This is formal and common in news. The causative passive uses ''have/get + object + past participle'' to say that we arrange for someone else to do something. For example, ''I had my car serviced'' means I arranged a mechanic to service it, not that I did it myself.

To avoid common mistakes, remember that the passive is formed with a form of ''be'' plus the past participle. Some verbs like ''arrive'' or ''die'' are intransitive and cannot be used in the passive. Also, be careful with prepositions: ''The doctor was sent for'' is correct, but ''The doctor was sent'' changes the meaning. Finally, don''t overuse the passive; it can sound impersonal if used too much.

Let''s practice these structures with the following questions and exercises. Each question will test your understanding of the advanced passive, and the exercises will give you hands-on practice. Remember, the goal is to make these structures a natural part of your English toolbox.', 'Which sentence correctly uses the passive with a modal verb?', '["The homework must be finished by tomorrow.","The homework must finish by tomorrow.","The homework must be finish by tomorrow.","The homework must finished by tomorrow."]'::jsonb, 0, '[{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"A forma correta do passivo com verbo modal é ''must be + particípio passado''. ''Be finished'' é a forma passiva do verbo ''finish''.","options":["The homework must be finished by tomorrow.","The homework must finish by tomorrow.","The homework must be finish by tomorrow.","The homework must finished by tomorrow."],"prompt":"Which sentence correctly uses the passive with a modal verb?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Futuro passivo: ''will be + particípio passado''. ''Built'' é o particípio passado de ''build''.","options":["will be built","will been built","will be build","is built"],"prompt":"Complete the sentence: ''A new hospital _______ in the area next year.''"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Present perfect passivo: ''has/have been + particípio passado''. ''Has been finished'' está correto.","options":["The report has been finished.","The report has being finished.","The report have been finished.","The report has be finished."],"prompt":"Choose the correct sentence in the present perfect passive."},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O passivo impessoal usa ''It is + particípio passado + that...'' para reportar opiniões. ''It is said that...'' está correto.","options":["It is said that the CEO will resign.","The CEO is said that he will resign.","People say the CEO to resign.","The CEO is said to be resigning."],"prompt":"Which sentence is in the impersonal passive?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A estrutura causativa ''have + something + done'' indica que você arranjou para outra pessoa fazer o serviço, não que você fez você mesmo.","options":["I repaired my computer myself.","I arranged for someone else to repair my computer.","My computer repaired itself.","I will repair my computer."],"prompt":"What does the sentence ''I had my computer repaired'' mean?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
