values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b2-bfa6ced6ada9', 'en', 'B2', 'Advanced Reported Speech: Shifting Perspectives', 'Have you ever found yourself in a situation where you needed to tell someone what another person said, but you were unsure whether to change the tense or the pronouns? At the B2 level, reported speech becomes more than just shifting verbs; it''s about accurately conveying the speaker''s original meaning while considering the context of the report. This skill is essential for both professional communication and casual conversation, especially when you need to share information without misrepresenting the source.

One of the fundamental concepts in advanced reported speech is backshifting. When you report a statement that was made in the past, you often move the tense one step back. For example, "I am feeling tired" becomes "She said she was feeling tired." However, if the information is still true or relevant at the time of reporting, you can keep the original tense. This is a common point of confusion, but the choice depends on the speaker''s perspective. If you are reporting something that remains a general truth, such as "The Earth revolves around the Sun," you might not change it to "revolved" if it''s still a fact.

Another critical aspect is the change in pronouns and time expressions. When reporting, you adjust words like ''today'' to ''that day'' or ''here'' to ''there''. For instance, if someone says, "I will meet you here tomorrow," and you report it later, you''d say, "He said he would meet me there the next day." These adjustments ensure that the reported speech makes sense in a new context, avoiding confusion about the timing or location.

Furthermore, questions and commands have their own reported forms. For questions, we use ''if'' or ''whether'' for yes/no questions, as in "She asked if I was coming." Commands are often reported using ''told'' or ''asked'' followed by an infinitive, like "He told me to close the door." These structures require attention because they don''t follow the same pattern as statements, but they are crucial for clear communication.

To go beyond the basics, you might also need to use reporting verbs other than ''say'' and ''tell'' to express the speaker''s intention more precisely. Verbs like ''suggest'', ''admit'', ''deny'', ''offer'', and ''warn'' can provide a richer context. For example, "He warned me not to touch the wire" sounds more natural than "He said to me not to touch the wire." Choosing the right reporting verb adds nuance and makes your speech more sophisticated.

Finally, remember that reported speech isn''t just about grammar; it''s about conveying the speaker''s tone and attitude. Whether you''re reporting a neutral statement or a strong opinion, your choices can affect how the message is received. As you practice, you''ll become more adept at making these adjustments instinctively. Now, let''s test your understanding with some exercises that focus on these advanced points.', 'Which of the following sentences correctly reports the statement: ''I am working on a new project''?', '["She said she was working on a new project.","She said she is working on a new project.","She said she worked on a new project.","She said she works on a new project."]'::jsonb, 0, '[{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"A forma correta de relatar uma afirmação no presente contínuo é mudar para o passado contínuo, a menos que a ação ainda esteja em andamento no momento da fala. A opção ''was working'' é a mais segura e correta gramaticalmente.","options":["She said she was working on a new project.","She said she is working on a new project.","She said she worked on a new project.","She said she works on a new project."],"prompt":"Which of the following sentences correctly reports the statement: ''I am working on a new project''?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Em perguntas com ''will'', ao relatar, transforma-se em ''would'' e a ordem da frase vira afirmativa. ''Asked if'' é a estrutura correta para perguntas de sim/não.","options":["He asked if I would join them for dinner.","He asked if I will join them for dinner.","He asked if I joined them for dinner.","He asked if I had joined them for dinner."],"prompt":"Choose the correct reported form for the question: ''Will you join us for dinner?''"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Para ordens negativas, usamos ''asked/told + pessoa + not to + verbo''. Além disso, ''here'' muda para ''there'' no relato. ''She asked me not to smoke there'' é a opção correta e natural.","options":["She asked me not to smoke there.","She asked me to not smoke here.","She said me not to smoke there.","She told to me not to smoke there."],"prompt":"Which sentence correctly reports the command: ''Please don''t smoke here''?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O passado perfeito no discurso indireto geralmente indica que o original estava no presente perfeito. Portanto, ''I have finished the report'' é a forma original correta.","options":["I have finished the report.","I had finished the report.","I finished the report.","I finish the report."],"prompt":"In the sentence ''He said that he had finished the report'', what is the original direct speech?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O verbo ''suggest'' é usado para relatar sugestões, e é seguido por ''that'' + sujeito + verbo no subjuntivo (ou ''should''). As outras opções não transmitem a ideia de sugestão.","options":["She suggested that we take a break.","She said that we take a break.","She told that we take a break.","She asked that we take a break."],"prompt":"Which sentence uses a reporting verb to convey the idea of suggestion?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
