insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b2-6a5905d5ffb8', 'en', 'B2', 'Mastering Past Perfect for Narratives', 'When telling stories in English, it''s common to describe events in the order they happened. But sometimes you need to talk about something that occurred before another past event. That''s where the past perfect tense comes in. It helps you make your narrative clearer and more precise.

The past perfect is formed with ''had'' plus the past participle of the verb. For example, ''I had finished my homework before I went out.'' This shows that the homework was completed before going out. You often use it with time expressions like ''when'', ''before'', ''after'', ''by the time'', and ''already''.

Imagine you are telling a friend about a trip you took. You might say, ''When I arrived at the station, the train had already left.'' The past perfect here (''had left'') shows that the train left before your arrival. Without it, the timeline would be confusing.

One common mistake is using the past perfect for actions that happened quickly one after another. For example, ''She had opened the door and walked in'' is not necessary. Use it only when you need to show that one action was completed before another.

Another tip: when you have two past actions, the one that happened first goes into past perfect, and the later one goes into simple past. For example, ''After I had eaten, I went for a walk.''

Practice with these questions to sharpen your skill. Each one presents a situation where you need to decide whether to use past perfect or another past tense.', 'Choose the correct sentence to describe the sequence: First, I ate. Then, I watched TV.', '["I watched TV after I had eaten.","I had watched TV after I ate.","I had eaten after I watched TV.","I ate after I had watched TV."]'::jsonb, 0, '[{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"A primeira ação (comer) deve estar no past perfect (''had eaten''), e a segunda (assistir TV) no simple past (''watched'').","options":["I watched TV after I had eaten.","I had watched TV after I ate.","I had eaten after I watched TV.","I ate after I had watched TV."],"prompt":"Choose the correct sentence to describe the sequence: First, I ate. Then, I watched TV."},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O past perfect é usado para indicar que a música parou antes de chegarmos. ''By the time'' + simple past, e a ação anterior no past perfect.","options":["the music had stopped","the music stopped","the music has stopped","the music stops"],"prompt":"Complete: By the time we arrived at the party, ______."},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"A primeira ação (ela sair) no past perfect ''had left'', e a segunda (eu chegar) no simple past ''arrived''. ''Already'' é usado com past perfect.","options":["She had already left when I arrived.","She already left when I had arrived.","She had already left when I had arrived.","She already left when I arrived."],"prompt":"Which sentence is correct?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"A razão (ver o filme) aconteceu antes da decisão de não ir, então usamos past perfect ''had seen''. O passado simples ''saw'' não mostraria essa anterioridade.","options":["had seen","saw","have seen","had saw"],"prompt":"Choose the best option: I didn''t go to the cinema because I ______ the movie before."},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Usamos past perfect para a ação que ocorreu primeiro (''had finished'') e simple past para a ação posterior (''went home'').","options":["After I had finished my work, I went home.","After I finished my work, I had gone home.","After I had finished my work, I had gone home.","After I finish my work, I had gone home."],"prompt":"Which sentence correctly uses past perfect?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
