values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b2-4620758caed0', 'en', 'B2', 'The Hidden History of Brazil''s Coffee Economy', 'Coffee is more than just a morning ritual for millions of Brazilians; it is a story of economic transformation and social change. While many people associate coffee with the bustling cafés of São Paulo or the rich aroma of a fresh brew, few realize how deeply this crop shaped the nation''s development. From the 19th century onward, coffee became the backbone of Brazil''s economy, and its influence can still be felt today.

The coffee boom began in the early 1800s, when the crop was introduced to the Paraíba Valley, a region spanning the states of Rio de Janeiro and São Paulo. The fertile soil and favorable climate proved ideal for cultivation, and soon coffee plantations, known as fazendas, spread across the landscape. By the mid-19th century, Brazil had become the world''s leading coffee producer, a position it has never relinquished.

The expansion of coffee cultivation was not without its costs. The industry relied heavily on slave labor until 1888, when slavery was abolished. After abolition, coffee growers faced a labor shortage and turned to European immigrants, particularly Italians and Japanese, who were attracted by promises of land and opportunity. These immigrants settled in the southern states, where they formed communities that still celebrate their heritage today.

The coffee economy also spurred urbanization and infrastructure development. Railroads were built to transport the beans from the interior to the ports, and cities like Santos and Rio de Janeiro grew rapidly as export hubs. Wealth from coffee financed the construction of grand buildings, theaters, and parks, transforming the architectural landscape of major cities. This era also saw the rise of a wealthy coffee oligarchy, whose members exerted considerable political influence, eventually leading to the ''café com leite'' (coffee with milk) policy, a power-sharing arrangement between the coffee-rich states of São Paulo and Minas Gerais.

However, the coffee boom was marked by volatility. Global prices fluctuated wildly, and overproduction often led to crises. In the early 20th century, the Brazilian government implemented price support programs, buying surplus coffee to maintain high prices, but this led to massive stockpiles and eventual waste. The Great Depression of 1929 dealt a severe blow to the industry, prompting a shift toward industrialization and economic diversification.

Today, Brazil remains the world''s largest coffee producer, but the industry has transformed. Modern farming techniques, sustainable practices, and a growing specialty coffee market have brought new opportunities and challenges. Smallholder farmers, many of whom are descendants of immigrants, now compete in a global marketplace. The legacy of coffee is complex, marked by both prosperity and exploitation, but its impact on Brazil''s identity is undeniable.

In recent decades, coffee culture in Brazil has evolved, with the rise of cafeterias and a new appreciation for the beverage''s nuances. Yet, the historical roots of this culture are often overlooked. Understanding the story of coffee in Brazil is essential to understanding the nation itself, as it weaves together themes of labor, immigration, economics, and politics. As you savor your next cup, take a moment to contemplate the journey those beans have taken, a journey that has shaped a country.', 'What was a significant consequence of the coffee boom in Brazil?', '["It led to the abolition of slavery.","It spurred the construction of railroads and urban growth.","It caused a decline in European immigration.","It resulted in the immediate diversification of the economy."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto afirma que a economia do café impulsionou o desenvolvimento de infraestrutura, como ferrovias, e o crescimento urbano, tornando a opção B correta.","options":["It led to the abolition of slavery.","It spurred the construction of railroads and urban growth.","It caused a decline in European immigration.","It resulted in the immediate diversification of the economy."],"prompt":"What was a significant consequence of the coffee boom in Brazil?","type":"reading"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto diz que o governo comprou o excedente de café para manter os preços altos, o que corresponde à opção C.","options":["It restricted coffee planting.","It encouraged farmers to switch to other crops.","It purchased surplus coffee to maintain prices.","It increased taxes on coffee exports."],"prompt":"How did the Brazilian government respond to overproduction of coffee in the early 20th century?","type":"reading"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto menciona que os produtores recorreram a imigrantes europeus, especialmente italianos e japoneses, após a abolição, tornando a opção B correta.","options":["They recruited laborers from other Brazilian states.","They brought in European immigrants.","They mechanized the harvesting process.","They reduced the size of their plantations."],"prompt":"After the abolition of slavery, how did coffee growers address the labor shortage?","type":"reading"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto explica que ''café com leite'' foi um acordo de poder entre os estados de São Paulo e Minas Gerais, portanto a opção C está correta.","options":["A program to support small coffee farmers.","A trade agreement with European countries.","A power-sharing arrangement between São Paulo and Minas Gerais.","A coffee-flavored milk drink popular in Brazil."],"prompt":"What is the ''café com leite'' policy mentioned in the text?","type":"reading"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"De acordo com o texto, a Grande Depressão causou um duro golpe na indústria do café e levou a uma mudança para a industrialização e diversificação econômica, tornando a opção C correta.","options":["It led to an increase in coffee exports.","It forced the government to end all support programs.","It accelerated the shift toward industrialization.","It caused coffee prices to rise sharply."],"prompt":"What impact did the Great Depression have on the Brazilian coffee industry?","type":"reading"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
