insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b2-973a24c5a49f', 'en', 'B2', 'Enriched Expressions: Participle Clauses in Action', 'Have you ever noticed how native speakers pack so much information into a single sentence? One secret is the participle clause. Learning to use it will make your English more concise, elegant, and natural. In this lesson, we''ll explore how to form and use participle clauses correctly, avoiding common pitfalls along the way.

First, let''s revisit the basics. A participle clause is a group of words that contains a present participle (e.g., ''walking'') or a past participle (e.g., ''walked'') and acts as an adjective or adverb. Unlike a full clause, it doesn''t have its own subject. Instead, it refers to the subject of the main clause. For example, in ''Walking home, I met an old friend,'' the participle clause ''Walking home'' describes what ''I'' was doing. It''s a way to combine two actions happening at the same time or to give background information.

There are two main types of participle clauses: present and past. Present participle clauses use the ''-ing'' form and often describe an action happening at the same time as the main verb, or they can explain a reason or result. For instance, ''Feeling tired, she went to bed early'' gives a reason. Past participle clauses use the ''-ed'' form (or irregular forms) and are often used in passive constructions. For example, ''Frightened by the noise, the cat hid under the sofa.'' This type often expresses a passive meaning or a state.

Now, let''s talk about usage. Participle clauses are common in written English, especially in formal or academic texts, but they also appear in speech. They can make your language more sophisticated. However, they require careful attention. A crucial rule is that the subject of the participle clause must be the same as the subject of the main clause. If they are different, you have a ''dangling modifier,'' which can cause confusion. For example, ''Walking down the street, the trees were beautiful'' is incorrect because the trees are not walking. Instead, you should say, ''Walking down the street, I thought the trees were beautiful.''

Another common mistake is getting the timing wrong. Sometimes, you need to use a perfect participle (e.g., ''having done'') to show that one action finished before another. For instance, ''Having finished my homework, I went out to play.'' This clarifies the sequence of events. Without the perfect form, the sentence could be ambiguous.

Let''s consider a few more examples. ''Used wisely, money can bring happiness'' uses a past participle to give a condition. ''The woman sitting by the window is my aunt'' uses a present participle to describe a noun. Notice that participle clauses can also replace relative clauses: ''The man who is talking to John'' becomes ''the man talking to John.'' This makes sentences shorter and more direct.

Now, let''s practice what we''ve learned. Pay close attention to the subjects and the timing. Are you ready? Let''s begin.', 'Which sentence correctly uses a present participle clause?', '["Walking down the street, the rain started.","Feeling hungry, I made a sandwich.","The book was interesting, reading it.","Having seen the film, the book was disappointing."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A única opção em que o sujeito da oração principal (''I'') é o mesmo da oração participial (''Feeling'') é a segunda. Nas outras, há um modificador solto (dangling modifier), pois o sujeito da oração principal não pode praticar a ação do particípio.","options":["Walking down the street, the rain started.","Feeling hungry, I made a sandwich.","The book was interesting, reading it.","Having seen the film, the book was disappointing."],"prompt":"Which sentence correctly uses a present participle clause?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"''Exhausted after the marathon'' é uma oração de particípio passado correta, descrevendo o sujeito ''I''. A opção B é uma frase fragmentada (sem verbo principal), C tem a forma errada (''frightening'' em vez de ''frightened''), e D é uma oração perfeita passiva, que não é exatamente um particípio passado simples.","options":["Exhausted after the marathon, I fell asleep.","The window broken by the wind.","Frightening by the noise, the cat ran away.","Having been written, the letter was sent."],"prompt":"Which sentence correctly uses a past participle clause?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Para mostrar que uma ação aconteceu antes da outra, usamos o particípio perfeito (''having + past participle''). ''Having finished'' indica que o trabalho foi concluído antes de ela ir para casa. ''Finishing'' implicaria simultaneidade, e as outras opções são gramaticalmente incorretas.","options":["Finishing her work, she went home.","Having finished her work, she went home.","Finished her work, she went home.","She finished her work, going home."],"prompt":"Choose the best way to combine these sentences using a participle clause: ''She finished her work. Then she went home.''"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Na opção C, o sujeito da oração principal é ''the question'', mas quem ''knows the answer'' é uma pessoa implícita. Isso cria um modificador solto (dangling modifier). As outras opções têm sujeitos compatíveis.","options":["Walking to the store, I saw a dog.","Opened carefully, the package revealed a gift.","Knowing the answer, the question was easy.","Having studied hard, she passed the exam."],"prompt":"Which sentence has a dangling modifier?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"A forma correta de substituir uma oração relativa ativa por um particípio presente é usar o gerúndio (''-ing''): ''the man standing...''. A opção B tem dois verbos principais, C usa ''being stood'' que é passivo e incorreto, e D usa o particípio passado, transmitindo uma ideia passiva.","options":["The man standing by the door is my uncle.","The man is standing by the door is my uncle.","The man being stood by the door is my uncle.","The man stood by the door is my uncle."],"prompt":"Select the sentence that correctly replaces the relative clause with a participle clause: ''The man who is standing by the door is my uncle.''"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
